(version 1)

# PicoEMP Rev-2026 custom design rules
#
# ORDER MATTERS: KiCad applies the LAST matching rule, not the first. Verified
# empirically -- a narrow exception placed before a broad rule is overridden by
# it, and the same exception placed after wins. So broad rules first, then the
# documented exceptions at the bottom of this file.
#
# ALSO: an unknown function in a condition is NOT an error. `A.memberOf('J1')`
# (the pre-v7 spelling) parses, reports nothing, and silently never matches, so
# the rule looks present while enforcing nothing. Every rule here was confirmed
# to actually fire against this board before being committed. If you add one,
# confirm the same way -- temporarily set an absurd constraint and check that
# violations naming your rule appear.
#
# Upstream annotates SCH-PICOEMP-REV04.PDF with:
#     ISOLATION BARRIER, 400V MIN.  >1MM CLEARANCE PER 61010-1.
#
# That rule governs the ISOLATION BARRIER -- HV side to logic side. It does not
# govern spacing *within* the HV section between two HV nets, which is bounded
# by component packages: an 0603 resistor has ~0.70 mm between its own pads, an
# 0805 ~0.80 mm, and the ATB3225 transformer 0.77 mm primary-to-secondary.
#
# Encoding the 1 mm figure as a plain netclass clearance is therefore wrong: it
# applies between any two differing nets touching the class, making 0603/0805
# parts illegal on HV nets. This rule scopes it correctly.
#
# Voltages, from primary sources (component ratings are headroom, not operating
# points -- C3 is 630 V rated, Q2 650 V):
#   HV capacitor charge      ~250 V   (firmware/micropython/cspico_simple.py)
#   Barrier design rating     400 V   (upstream schematic annotation)
#   Hi-pot proof test           1 kV  ("well beyond the voltages the device can
#                                      generate" -- hardware/design_notes)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Broad rules
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# hasNetclass() rather than `A.NetClass == 'HV'` so the barrier cannot develop a
# hole if a net ever picks up an additional class.
(rule "HV isolation barrier"
	(constraint clearance (min 1.0mm))
	(condition "A.hasNetclass('HV') && !B.hasNetclass('HV')"))

# HV copper should also keep away from the exposed cut edge. Scoped to TRACKS
# and VIAS only -- i.e. to routed copper, where the designer has a choice.
#
# Pads are excluded deliberately: J1 and J4 are edge-mount SMAs and their pads
# are *required* to reach the board edge; that is what an edge-launch connector
# is. An earlier version of this rule applied to all HV items and flagged J1 as
# a violation, which was the rule being wrong rather than the layout.
(rule "HV routed copper to board edge"
	(constraint edge_clearance (min 1.0mm))
	(condition "A.hasNetclass('HV') && (A.Type == 'Track' || A.Type == 'Via')"))

# The barrier rule above is scoped HV-to-non-HV, so it deliberately says nothing
# about spacing *between* two HV nets. That is right in general -- intra-HV
# spacing is bounded by package geometry -- but it leaves three pairs unguarded
# that actually stand at the full rail voltage:
#
#   HV_RTN <-> HV_SENSE   ~246 V   (SW3 / J3 contacts, and the R1 + Q1 LED branch)
#   HV_RTN <-> HV_RAIL     250 V   (across C3)
#   HV_RTN <-> HV_OUT      250 V   (Q2 collector-emitter, when the IGBT is off)
#
# Everything else on the HV side sits within a few volts of its neighbour
# (R2 drops 3.7 V; D7 clamps the gate to 18 V), so those pairs need nothing
# beyond the default class.
#
# IPC-2221A Table 6-1 B1 (external, uncoated, <=3050 m) requires 0.40 mm at
# 171-300 V. The board currently achieves 0.498 mm at its worst point, so it is
# compliant with ~25% margin -- but that is thinner than anywhere else on the HV
# side, and thinner than you would pick deliberately for a 246 V gap.
#
# 0.8 mm below is a TARGET, not the current state. As of this writing it reports
# 9 violations, worst first:
#
#   0.498 mm  HV_RTN trunk track  <->  SW3 pad 2 (HV_SENSE)   x3 track segments
#   0.514 -
#   0.552 mm  HV_RTN trunk track  <->  HV_SENSE teardrops     x5
#   0.648 mm  HV_RTN track        <->  C3 pad 2 (HV_RAIL)
#
# Severity is `warning` for that reason -- it should inform a layout decision,
# not block the fab gate on a board that already meets IPC.
#
# The governing number, 0.498 mm, is pad-to-track and has nothing to do with
# teardrops: the HV_RTN trunk running down x = 124.39 simply passes close to
# SW3's HV_SENSE pad at (122.942, 149.664). Disabling teardrops on the HV nets
# does NOT fix it -- that was tried and measured. Closing this properly means
# nudging that trunk east of SW3, which is interactive work in pcbnew.
#
# Confirmed to fire: temporarily raising the constraint to 5 mm produced
# violations naming this rule, per the procedure in the header above.
(rule "Intra-HV spacing at full rail voltage"
	(severity warning)
	(constraint clearance (min 0.8mm))
	(condition "A.NetName == '/HV_RTN' && (B.NetName == '/HV_SENSE' || B.NetName == '/HV_RAIL' || B.NetName == '/HV_OUT')"))

# Advisory, not blocking. HV copper carries a 250 V rail and, on the discharge
# loop, a very high dI/dt pulse; 0.2 mm signal track is thin for both. Necking
# below this at a pad is legitimate and KiCad has no auto-neck, so this is a
# warning you can leave standing rather than an error that stops a route.
(rule "HV track width advisory"
	(severity warning)
	(constraint track_width (min 0.4mm))
	(condition "A.hasNetclass('HV') && A.Type == 'Track'"))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Documented exceptions -- must stay BELOW the broad rules to take effect
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# J1 and J4 are Amphenol 132289 edge-mount SMAs. Their pads straddle the board
# edge by design: the connector clamps the 1.6 mm edge and the pads must run out
# to it. The global 0.5 mm copper-to-edge constraint is meaningless for them.
(rule "Edge-mount SMA pads may reach the board edge"
	(constraint edge_clearance (min 0mm))
	(condition "A.memberOfFootprint('J1') || A.memberOfFootprint('J4')"))

# T1/T2 are ATB322524-0110 transformers. Primary-to-secondary spacing inside the
# package is 0.770 mm, below the 1 mm barrier. That is the part's own geometry,
# not a routing choice, and upstream shipped and hi-pot tested it. Scoped to pad
# pairs within one package so it cannot relax anything the designer controls.
(rule "ATB3225 intra-package pad spacing - T1"
	(constraint clearance (min 0.75mm))
	(condition "A.memberOfFootprint('T1') && B.memberOfFootprint('T1')"))

(rule "ATB3225 intra-package pad spacing - T2"
	(constraint clearance (min 0.75mm))
	(condition "A.memberOfFootprint('T2') && B.memberOfFootprint('T2')"))

# Q2 (TO-252 / DPAK) has NO exception here, deliberately. UNRESOLVED - see below.
#
# DRC reports 0.080 mm between Q2 pads 1-2 and 2-3. I could not reconcile that
# with the footprint geometry, which reads as 1.08 mm: the lead pads are
# (size 2.2 1.2) on a 2.28 mm pitch, so along the pitch axis 2.28 - 1.2 = 1.08.
# The board's footprint is byte-identical to KiCad's stock library copy, so
# nothing here is corrupted, and 2.2 mm along the pitch axis would make adjacent
# DPAK leads nearly touch -- physically wrong for the package.
#
# An earlier version of this file carried a 0.07 mm exception for Q2, written on
# the assumption that 0.080 mm was real package geometry. That was removed. Pads
# 2 and 3 are the IGBT collector and emitter and stand at ~250 V; quietly
# relaxing clearance there to 0.07 mm on an unverified number is the one failure
# mode worth avoiding most. A false positive costs annoyance, a false negative
# costs a flashover.
#
# RESOLVE BEFORE FAB: open Q2 in the footprint editor and measure the lead pads
# directly. If the spacing really is 1.08 mm, this violation is a DRC artefact
# and can be excluded in the GUI with that measurement recorded. If it really is
# 0.080 mm, the footprint is unusable at 250 V and Q2 needs a different one.
